## Proof

**Claim:** Yes, it is possible to construct such a function $f$, even when the number of equivalence classes is uncountable.

**Construction.**

Let $\sim$ be an equivalence relation on a set $X$, and let $X/{\sim}$ denote the set of all equivalence classes:
$$X/{\sim} \;=\; \{\, [x]_{\sim} : x \in X \,\}, \qquad [x]_{\sim} = \{\, y \in X : y \sim x \,\}.$$

This is a well-defined set (a subset of $\mathcal{P}(X)$). Define the **canonical quotient map**
$$f : X \longrightarrow X/{\sim}, \qquad f(x) = [x]_{\sim}.$$

**Verification of "$f(x)=f(y) \iff x\sim y$":**

($\Longrightarrow$) Suppose $f(x) = f(y)$, i.e., $[x]_{\sim} = [y]_{\sim}$. Since $x \in [x]_{\sim} = [y]_{\sim}$, we have $x \sim y$.

($\Longleftarrow$) Suppose $x \sim y$. Then $[x]_{\sim} = [y]_{\sim}$ by the definition of equivalence classes (any two $\sim$-equivalent elements generate the same class). Hence $f(x) = f(y)$.

**Why the uncountability of $X/{\sim}$ poses no obstacle:**

The construction above requires only:
1. That $X/{\sim}$ is a **set** — guaranteed since it is a subset of the power set $\mathcal{P}(X)$, which exists by the Power Set axiom.
2. That the map $x \mapsto [x]_{\sim}$ is well-defined — guaranteed since equivalence classes partition $X$, so every $x$ belongs to exactly one class.

Neither step uses any cardinality restriction. The codomain $X/{\sim}$ may be finite, countably infinite, or uncountable; the quotient map is defined identically in all cases. In particular:

- The Power Set axiom gives us $\mathcal{P}(X)$ regardless of cardinality.
- Separation yields $X/{\sim} \subseteq \mathcal{P}(X)$.
- The function $f$ is given by an explicit first-order formula, so Replacement/Specification is not even needed beyond what is already available.

No form of the Axiom of Choice is required here: we are not selecting representatives from each class (which would require Choice when the index set is arbitrary). We are mapping each element to its *entire* class, which is canonical and choice-free.

**Conclusion.** The canonical quotient map $f(x) = [x]_{\sim}$ is a function with domain $X$ satisfying $f(x) = f(y) \iff x \sim y$, and it exists for **any** equivalence relation on **any** set $X$, including when the number of equivalence classes is uncountable.

